SEG = 'REG'
CAT = 'cpbRegs'
Q = [
('Who maintains the NCCI edits used for Medicare and Medicaid claims?',
 'CMS', ['The AMA', 'Each MAC separately', 'The HHS OIG'],
 'CMS developed and maintains the National Correct Coding Initiative edits for Medicare and the Medicaid NCCI edits that state programs use.'),
('NCCI procedure-to-procedure edits compare services reported by:',
 'Same provider, patient and date', ['Any provider in the same city', 'The same patient over 30 days', 'Same facility, any patient'],
 'NCCI PTP edits apply to services billed by the same provider for the same beneficiary on the same date of service (CMS NCCI edits pages).'),
("A MAC's LCD covers a test in its jurisdiction. Does that LCD bind a MAC in another jurisdiction?",
 'No, only its own jurisdiction', ['Yes, all MACs must follow it', 'Yes, once CMS posts it online', 'Only for Medicare Advantage plans'],
 'An LCD applies only within the jurisdiction of the MAC that wrote it; a national coverage determination is what binds every MAC.'),
('A national coverage determination (NCD) applies:',
 'Nationwide to Medicare', ["Only in the MAC's region", 'Only to Medicare Advantage', 'Only after an appeal'],
 "An NCD limits or sets coverage of a service on a national basis for Medicare; an LCD applies in one contractor's area."),
('Under incident-to rules in a physician office, where must the supervising physician be?',
 'In the suite, available', ['In the same room as the aide', 'Reachable by phone or text', 'In the office, any floor'],
 'Direct supervision in the office means the physician is present in the office suite and immediately available, not necessarily in the room.'),
('A nurse makes a home visit alone and gives an injection. Can it be billed as incident to the physician?',
 'No, there is no direct supervision', ['Yes, if the physician ordered it', "Yes, if billed under the nurse's NPI", 'No, injections are never covered'],
 'Outside the office, incident-to coverage needs direct physician supervision; a nurse visiting alone does not meet it (Benefit Policy Manual ch. 15, 60.1).'),
('Who may be the auxiliary personnel for an incident-to service?',
 'Employee or contractor', ['Only a W-2 employee', 'Only a licensed nurse', 'Only staff the hospital hired'],
 'Auxiliary personnel may be an employee, leased employee or independent contractor of the physician or billing entity.'),
('A drug is bought by the patient and given in the office. What can be billed incident to?',
 'The administration only', ['The drug and its administration', 'The drug only', 'Neither the drug nor the shot'],
 'Supplies must be an expense to the billing physician; a patient-bought drug is not covered, but giving it is.'),
('A major surgery with a 090 global indicator is done on March 1. Related follow-up visits are in the package through which day?',
 'Day 90 after surgery', ['Day 30 after surgery', 'Day 10 after surgery', 'Day 60 after surgery'],
 'A 090 global period covers the day before, the day of, and the 90 days immediately after a major surgery (Claims Processing Manual ch. 12).'),
('For a minor procedure with a 10-day global period, which visit is included in its payment?',
 'A related follow-up on day 6', ['An unrelated visit on day 6', 'A follow-up on day 12', 'The initial decision visit for major surgery'],
 'With a 010 indicator, postoperative visits related to recovery within 10 days are not paid separately.'),
('A procedure has a global indicator of 000. Which is true about visits after the day of the procedure?',
 'They are paid separately', ['They are bundled for 10 days', 'They are bundled for 90 days', 'They need modifier 78'],
 'With a 000 entry, postoperative visits beyond the day of the procedure are not in the payment and are paid separately.'),
('Which service is outside the global surgical package and paid separately?',
 'Diagnostic tests and imaging', ['Removal of sutures', 'Dressing changes', 'Postsurgical pain management by the surgeon'],
 'Diagnostic tests and procedures are excluded from the package; suture removal, dressing changes and pain care by the surgeon are included.'),
('A complication sends the patient back to the operating room during the global period. How is it treated?',
 'Payable separately', ['Included in the package', 'Billed to the patient', 'Never payable'],
 'Treatment of complications that requires a return to the operating room is not part of the global package.'),
('A complication is treated at the bedside by the surgeon during the global period. How is it treated?',
 'Included in the package', ['Paid as a new procedure', 'Paid with modifier 79', 'Billed to a second payer'],
 "Complications that don't require a return trip to the operating room are covered by the global payment."),
('Two procedures that could not reasonably be done at the same session are billed together. Which kind of NCCI edit does this hit?',
 'A mutually exclusive edit', ['A medically unlikely edit', 'A frequency limit edit', 'A gender-specific edit'],
 'NCCI PTP edits include code pairs that are mutually exclusive, services that cannot reasonably be performed at the same session; MUEs instead cap units of one code.'),
('Under ASCA, which physician practice may still send Medicare claims on paper?',
 'One with fewer than 10 FTEs', ['One with fewer than 25 FTEs', 'One billing under $1 million a year', 'One with no EHR system'],
 'ASCA requires electronic Medicare claims, but a physician, practitioner or supplier with fewer than 10 full-time equivalent employees is a small provider that may bill on paper; the 25-FTE threshold is for institutional providers.'),
('A hospital outpatient department bills its facility charges on paper. Which form does it use?',
 'UB-04', ['CMS-1500', 'CMS-R-131', 'CMS-855B'],
 'Institutional providers bill on Form CMS-1450, the UB-04, or the 837I electronic equivalent.'),
('Under ASCA, how must most Medicare claims be sent?',
 'Electronically', ['On paper', 'By fax', 'Through the beneficiary'],
 'Electronic billing is required except where an ASCA exception applies (Claims Processing Manual ch. 1).'),
('In the Medicare Shared Savings Program, what does an ACO that lowers spending and meets quality standards receive?',
 'A share of the savings', ['A higher conversion factor', 'Exemption from MSP rules', 'A larger GPCI for its area'],
 'Shared Savings Program ACOs that meet quality performance standards and lower growth in Medicare spending can share in the savings they generate.'),
("A commercial payer's policy differs from Medicare's on a modifier. Which rule applies to that payer's claims?",
 "The payer's own policy", ["Medicare's rule always", 'The stricter of the two', "The provider's choice"],
 'Commercial payers set their own payment policies in their contracts; Medicare rules bind Medicare claims.'),
('Medicare does not pay an E/M visit with modifier 57 if the procedure the next day has which global period?',
 '10 days', ['90 days', 'Major surgery', 'YYY carrier-priced'],
 'Modifier 57 is paid only for the decision for a procedure with a 90-day global period, not 0- or 10-day procedures.'),
('Two surgeons agree that one does the surgery and the other the postoperative care. What documents the transfer?',
 'A written agreement', ['A verbal note on the superbill', 'A signed ABN for the visits', 'A transfer modifier on each claim'],
 'Services of other physicians are separate when they agree on the transfer of care, in a letter or an annotation in the record.'),
("A code's global indicator is ZZZ. What does that mean?",
 "Add-on; uses primary's global", ['A 90-day major surgery', 'A carrier-priced global period', 'No global and not an add-on'],
 "ZZZ codes are add-on surgical codes always billed with another service; the primary code's global period applies."),
('A patient saw a cardiologist 2 years ago and now sees a dermatologist in the same group. For Medicare, which visit type applies?',
 'New patient visit', ['Established patient visit', 'Consultation visit', 'Preventive visit only'],
 'The 3-year established rule counts only services from the same physician or another physician of the exact same specialty and subspecialty in the group (Claims Processing Manual ch. 12, 30.6.7).'),
]
